                               Review by liggitt                                

Summary                                                                         

3 comments to consider below.                                                   

Inline Comments                                                                 

pkg/controller/podautoscaler/metrics/utilization.go (confidence: 0.85)          

▌ case percentage.Cmp(big.NewInt(math.MinInt32)) < 0: currentUtilization =    
▌ math.MinInt32                                                               

can metric.Value or a request actually be negative by the time we get here? if  
not, this branch is unreachable and the comment above should say the percentage 
is non-negative so the clamp is one-sided... if it can, none of the four new    
table rows exercise it, so add a row that drives the percentage below           
math.MinInt32 (e.g. a single pod with Value: -(1 << 40) and request 1) so the   
lower rail is actually demonstrated rather than assumed                         

pkg/controller/podautoscaler/metrics/utilization.go (confidence: 0.70)          

▌ return float64(currentUtilization) / float64(targetUtilization),            
▌ currentUtilization, rawAverageValue, nil                                    

the ratio is now computed from the clamped int32, not the exact percentage...   
with target 50 and an exact percentage of 2^40, the ratio returned is ~4.3e7    
instead of ~2.2e10. that is almost certainly fine in practice since the replica 
calculator caps the result anyway, but it means utilizationRatio and            
currentUtilization are consistent with each other while both being wrong        
relative to the real inputs. if that is the intended behavior (and I think it   
is, given the release note), say so in the comment above the switch so the next 
person doesn't "fix" it by computing the ratio from percentage directly and     
desynchronizing the two                                                         

pkg/controller/podautoscaler/metrics/utilization.go (confidence: 0.55)          

▌ rawAverageValue = metricsTotal.Quo(metricsTotal,                            
▌ big.NewInt(numEntries)).Int64()                                             

nit: this mutates metricsTotal in place, which is fine only because nothing     
reads it after this line... new(big.Int).Quo(metricsTotal,                      
big.NewInt(numEntries)).Int64() makes that not depend on statement ordering. no 
need to change unless you're touching this for other reasons                    
